    <fr:title text="The q-Steenrod operations">The q-Steenrod operations</fr:title>
    <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
    <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>The <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>-Steenrod operations: the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k]]></fr:tex>-algebra homomorphism <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \mathcal {P} \colon  S \to  S[T], \qquad  x \mapsto  x + x^q T
]]></fr:tex>
on linear forms, with <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}(f) = \sum _{i \ge  0} \mathcal {P}^{i}(f) T^i]]></fr:tex>.
Each <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}^{i}]]></fr:tex> raises degree by <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[i(q-1)]]></fr:tex> and preserves the
invariants of any degree-preserving action. For homogeneous <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> of
degree <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d]]></fr:tex> with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {F}_q]]></fr:tex>-coefficients, <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \mathcal {P}^{i}(f) = 0 \text { for } i > d,
  \qquad  \mathcal {P}^{d}(f) = f^q.
]]></fr:tex>
These two vanishing facts are what make <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0005/" title="The Steenrod ideal" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0005/" display-uri="ffi-0005" type="local">the Steenrod ideal</fr:link>
finitely generated in the stated degrees.</html:p>
    <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">Setup: conjugation over a finite field</fr:link>.</html:p>
    <html:p>
      <html:em>Provenance: Maithani 2025; L. Smith, <html:em>Polynomial Invariants of Finite
Groups</html:em>, Ch.~11.</html:em>
    </html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
