            <fr:title text="Setup: conjugation over a finite field">Setup: conjugation over a finite field</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Fix a prime power <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>, let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k = \mathbb {F}_q]]></fr:tex> and let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K = \overline {k}]]></fr:tex>.
Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S = k[x_{ij} : 1 \le  i, j \le  n]]]></fr:tex> be the coordinate ring of
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathrm {M}_n = \mathrm {M}_{n \times  n}]]></fr:tex>, with generic matrix <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X = (x_{ij})]]></fr:tex>,
carrying the conjugation action of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[G = \mathrm {GL}_n(k)]]></fr:tex>: the element
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\sigma  \in  G]]></fr:tex> maps the variables to the entries of
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\sigma ^{-1} X \sigma ]]></fr:tex>. The kernel of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[G \to  \mathrm {GL}(\mathrm {M}_n(k))]]></fr:tex> is the
scalars, so the effective group is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\widehat {G} \cong  \mathrm {PGL}_n(k)]]></fr:tex>, and
the action is modular — not every invariant of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathrm {M}_n(k)]]></fr:tex> comes from
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S^G]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p>The characteristic polynomial of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex>: <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \chi _{X}(\lambda ) = \lambda ^n - c_{1} \lambda ^{n-1}
  + c_{2} \lambda ^{n-2} - \cdots  + (-1)^n c_{n},
  \qquad  c_{j} \in  S^G, \quad  \deg  c_{j} = j.
]]></fr:tex>
So <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c_{1} = \operatorname {tr} X]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c_{n} = \det  X]]></fr:tex>, and
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S^G = k[c_{1}, \dots , c_{n}]]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p>For <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[M \in  \mathrm {M}_n(K)]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F(M)]]></fr:tex> is the entrywise Frobenius
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(m_{ij}) \mapsto  (m_{ij}^q)]]></fr:tex>. Since the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>-power map is a ring
endomorphism of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F]]></fr:tex> is a ring endomorphism of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathrm {M}_n(K)]]></fr:tex>,
bijective because Frobenius is surjective on the algebraically closed
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K]]></fr:tex>; in particular <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F]]></fr:tex> preserves and reflects nilpotency. Write
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {N} \subset  \mathrm {M}_n(K)]]></fr:tex> for the nilpotent cone.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: distilled.tex Part 1; journal sec:setup.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
